"""
Derived from circleGenSquish.py

Generate a G-code file for a hot-wire (2D, X/Y only) cutter that cuts
circles out of material fed in from a roll/stock sheet by a Z-axis
feeder, using a grid or hex layout of a main circle size - AND tries to
fill the leftover margin at the start and end of every row with a
smaller circle instead of wasting that strip of material.

Everything about how a SINGLE row (or a single "plane" of rows) is laid
out and cut is unchanged from circleGenSquish.py: segments, margin
filling, SQUISH_ROOM insets on X, and the SEVER_DISTANCE safety cut.
What's new here is the MACHINE / MATERIAL-HANDLING layer on top of that,
to deal with the fact that the material is far longer than the cutting
area and has to be fed in by a physical pusher.

MACHINE LAYOUT
  The X/Y hot-wire gantry cuts in a normal 2D plane, same as before.
  The Z axis is a FEEDER, not a cutting axis - it physically pushes the
  material stock onto the cutting bed. The feeder shares its line of
  travel with the Y axis: think of one straight rail with a homing
  switch at each end.

    Y0                     <- Y axis home switch (front of cutting bed)
    |
    v Y+                   <- Y increases toward the middle
    |
    |
    ^ Z+                   <- Z increases toward the middle
    |
    Z0                     <- Z axis (feeder) home switch

  Y0 and Z0 are Z_Y_AXIS_OFFSET mm apart (the true, empty-rail distance
  between the two homing switches - this has to be bigger than
  MATERIAL_LENGTH, or the stock physically couldn't be loaded between
  them), and Y+/Z+ both point toward each other (toward the middle of
  that span). The wire's Y travel is hard-limited to Y_MAX (an absolute
  mechanical limit - the wire should NEVER actually be commanded that
  far out; real cutting happens in a much smaller window near Y0,
  governed by PLANE_HEIGHT).

  The stock is loaded fully extended: the feeder grips its TRAILING
  edge at its home position (Z0, i.e. Z=0), so the LEADING edge sits at
  Z=MATERIAL_LENGTH before any feed motion happens. Measured the other
  way (distance from Y0, which is what matters for reaching it with the
  wire), that's:

      initial_s = Z_Y_AXIS_OFFSET - MATERIAL_LENGTH

  If initial_s is still further out than Y_MAX, there's a stretch of
  feed travel that just pushes the (still unreachable) leading edge
  closer, before the wire can usefully reach it at all:

      UNUSABLE_SPACE = initial_s - Y_MAX
                      = (Z_Y_AXIS_OFFSET - MATERIAL_LENGTH) - Y_MAX

  Both are NOT hard-coded - they're derived from Z_Y_AXIS_OFFSET,
  MATERIAL_LENGTH and Y_MAX so they stay correct if any of those
  machine/material constants change.

FEED / CUT CYCLE
  1. FIRST feed only: push the material forward by
       UNUSABLE_SPACE + ORIGIN_Y
     ORIGIN_Y doubles as the "safety distance" you described (the gap
     between the Y0 switch and where the wire is actually allowed to
     start cutting, e.g. Y10) - linking the two means there's only one
     number to configure, and the feed math and the cutting math always
     agree on where "the front of the cutting window" is.
  2. Cut the row layout for one cutting window (PLANE_HEIGHT tall, same
     compute_rows/compute_row_segments logic as circleGenSquish.py).
     Because ORIGIN_Y, PLANE_HEIGHT, SQUISH_ROOM etc. never change, this
     window - and the row layout inside it - is IDENTICAL every single
     cycle. Only the material underneath is different each time.
  3. Send the wire home (X0, Y0) to get it clear of the bed.
  4. Push the material forward again, this time by exactly the span the
     wire just cut through (front-most point the wire reached to
     back-most point it reached, which includes each circle's radius,
     not just the row centerline). That's precisely enough fresh,
     never-cut material to fill the window for the next cycle.
  5. Repeat from step 2 until the material's TRAILING edge would end up
     within SQUISH_ROOM of the deepest point the wire cuts to - i.e.
     there's no longer a safe amount of uncut stock left behind the cut
     line to support another full cycle.

Adjust the VARIABLES section below.

Run:
    python circleGenFeeder.py
Output:
    circles.gcode
"""

import math

# ---------------- VARIABLES ----------------
PLANE_WIDTH = 1200.0        # mm, width of the material to cut from (X direction)
PLANE_HEIGHT = 200.0        # mm, height of ONE cutting window (Y direction) - how
                            # much depth the wire actually cuts into per feed cycle.
                            # NOT the same thing as Y_MAX below.

SQUISH_ROOM = 2.0          # mm, the material can squish/stretch/shift a little.
                            # Circles are inset SQUISH_ROOM from every nominal
                            # edge so they're never partial. Straight waste
                            # cuts at the left/right edges are driven
                            # SQUISH_ROOM past the nominal edge so they fully
                            # separate the block regardless. Also reserved as
                            # a safety inset at the very tail end of the sheet
                            # (MATERIAL_LENGTH) so the last cycle isn't cut
                            # flush with an uncertain trailing edge.
CIRCLE_DIAMETER = 90.0     # mm, main circle size used for the grid/hex layout
SEVER_DISTANCE = 2.0       # mm, the circles could stay hanging by a thin, uncut piece of material, make sure to cut that, just in case

PATTERN = "hex"           # "grid" or "hex" - layout used for the main circles

DIAMETER_OPTIONS = [70, 50.0, 30.0]
# List of acceptable diameters to check against when a margin has leftover
# space. The script picks the LARGEST one from this list that still fits.
# Include CIRCLE_DIAMETER in here too if you want it considered for
# margins as well as the main layout (it usually won't fit there, but
# there's no harm including it).

FEED_RATE = 300            # mm/min, speed while cutting
TRAVEL_RATE = 500          # mm/min, speed while travelling (non-productive moves)
PUSH_RATE = 500             # mm/min, speed of the Z-axis feeder while pushing material

ORIGIN_X = 10.0             # mm, X offset of the plane's bottom-left corner
ORIGIN_Y = 10.0             # mm, Y offset of the cutting window's front edge.
                            # Also doubles as the feeder "safety distance":
                            # how far past the Y0 switch the material's front
                            # edge is parked before the wire is allowed to
                            # start cutting.

# --- machine / material-handling constants (new) ---
Z_Y_AXIS_OFFSET = 398.035   # mm, distance between the Y0 and Z0 homing switches.
                            # Y+ and Z+ both point toward the middle of this span.
Y_MAX = 280.0               # mm, absolute hard travel limit of the Y axis. The
                            # wire should never actually be commanded this far -
                            # real cutting happens within PLANE_HEIGHT of ORIGIN_Y.
MATERIAL_LENGTH = 2000.0    # mm, total length of the material sheet along the
                            # feed (Z) direction.

OUTPUT_FILE = "circles.gcode"
# --------------------------------------------


def best_fit_diameter(diameters, available):
    """Largest diameter from the list that fits within `available` space."""
    candidates = [d for d in diameters if d <= available + 1e-9]
    if not candidates:
        return None
    return max(candidates)


def compute_rows(plane_height, diameter, pattern):
    """Return a list of (row_y, is_offset) for every row that fits vertically."""
    radius = diameter / 2

    if pattern == "grid":
        row_height = diameter
    elif pattern == "hex":
        row_height = diameter * math.sqrt(3) / 2
    else:
        raise ValueError("PATTERN must be 'grid' or 'hex'")

    rows = []
    i = 0
    while True:
        y = radius + i * row_height
        if y + radius > plane_height + 1e-9:
            break
        is_offset = (pattern == "hex" and i % 2 == 1)
        rows.append((y, is_offset))
        i += 1

    return rows


def compute_row_segments(plane_width, main_diameter, is_offset, diameter_options):
    """
    Build the left-to-right list of segments for one row.
    Each segment is a tuple:
      ("straight", x_start, x_end)
      ("circle",   x_start, x_end, diameter)
    """
    radius = main_diameter / 2
    left_margin = radius if is_offset else 0.0

    available = plane_width - left_margin
    circle_count = max(0, math.floor(available / main_diameter + 1e-9))
    main_end = left_margin + circle_count * main_diameter
    right_margin = plane_width - main_end

    segments = []
    x = 0.0

    # --- leading margin: try to fill with the largest diameter that fits ---
    fill_d = best_fit_diameter(diameter_options, left_margin)
    if fill_d:
        waste = left_margin - fill_d
        if waste > 1e-9:
            segments.append(("straight", x, x + waste))
            x += waste
        segments.append(("circle", x, x + fill_d, fill_d))
        x += fill_d
    else:
        segments.append(("straight", x, x + left_margin))
        x += left_margin

    # --- main circles ---
    for _ in range(circle_count):
        segments.append(("circle", x, x + main_diameter, main_diameter))
        x += main_diameter

    # --- trailing margin: try to fill with the largest diameter that fits ---
    fill_d = best_fit_diameter(diameter_options, right_margin)
    if fill_d:
        segments.append(("circle", x, x + fill_d, fill_d))
        x += fill_d
        waste = plane_width - x
        if waste > 1e-9:
            segments.append(("straight", x, x + waste))
            x = plane_width
    else:
        segments.append(("straight", x, plane_width))
        x = plane_width

    return segments


def place_segments_in_plane(segments, plane_width, squish_room):
    """
    Take row segments that were planned on the shrunk "effective" width
    (0 .. plane_width - 2*squish_room) and place them into real plane
    coordinates (0 .. plane_width), by:

      1. Shifting everything right by squish_room, so every circle ends
         up at least squish_room away from the real X=0 / X=plane_width
         edges (perfect circles, no missing chunk).
      2. Extending whatever straight segment borders each outer edge so
         it runs squish_room PAST the nominal edge instead of stopping
         on it - this is the cut that severs the block from the scrap
         around it, so it needs to reach the real edge even if the real
         edge is up to squish_room further out than nominal. If a side
         has no straight segment to extend (e.g. a filler circle landed
         flush on x=0), a small straight overcut segment is inserted so
         that buffer strip still gets fully cut through.
    """
    shifted = []
    for seg in segments:
        if seg[0] == "straight":
            shifted.append(("straight", seg[1] + squish_room, seg[2] + squish_room))
        else:
            _, x_start, x_end, d = seg
            shifted.append(("circle", x_start + squish_room, x_end + squish_room, d))

    if not shifted:
        return shifted

    # --- left edge: reach to -squish_room instead of stopping at 0 ---
    left_target = -squish_room
    if shifted[0][0] == "straight":
        shifted[0] = ("straight", left_target, shifted[0][2])
    elif shifted[0][1] > left_target + 1e-9:
        shifted.insert(0, ("straight", left_target, shifted[0][1]))

    # --- right edge: reach to plane_width + squish_room instead of plane_width ---
    right_target = plane_width + squish_room
    if shifted[-1][0] == "straight":
        shifted[-1] = ("straight", shifted[-1][1], right_target)
    elif shifted[-1][2] < right_target - 1e-9:
        shifted.append(("straight", shifted[-1][2], right_target))

    return shifted


def row_has_circles(segments):
    return any(seg[0] == "circle" for seg in segments)


def emit_row(lines, row_y, segments):
    """Append the G-code for one full row sweep (there and back)."""
    abs_y = ORIGIN_Y + row_y
    row_start_x = ORIGIN_X + segments[0][1]  # may be < ORIGIN_X due to left overcut

    # Travel to the (possibly squish-overcut) left edge of the row
    lines.append(f"G1 X{row_start_x:.3f} Y{abs_y:.3f} F{TRAVEL_RATE} ; travel to left edge of row")

    # Forward sweep: cut every segment, left to right
    for seg in segments:
        if seg[0] == "straight":
            x_end = ORIGIN_X + seg[2]
            lines.append(f"G1 X{x_end:.3f} Y{abs_y:.3f} F{FEED_RATE} ; cut straight (margin/gap)")
        else:
            _, x_start, x_end, d = seg
            r = d / 2
            lines.append(
                f"G3 X{ORIGIN_X + x_end:.3f} Y{abs_y:.3f} I{r:.3f} J0.000 F{FEED_RATE} "
                f"; cut lower half of circle d={d:g}"
            )

    # Reverse sweep: travel back over straights, cut upper half of every circle
    for seg in reversed(segments):
        if seg[0] == "straight":
            x_start = ORIGIN_X + seg[1]
            lines.append(f"G1 X{x_start:.3f} Y{abs_y:.3f} F{TRAVEL_RATE} ; travel back over margin/gap")
        else:
            _, x_start, x_end, d = seg
            r = d / 2
            lines.append(
                f"G3 X{ORIGIN_X + x_start:.3f} Y{abs_y:.3f} I{-r:.3f} J0.000 F{FEED_RATE} "
                f"; cut upper half of circle d={d:g}"
            )

            # make an additional straight cut to fully sever the circle, just in case
            lines.append(
                f"G1 X{ORIGIN_X + x_start:.3f} Y{(abs_y - SEVER_DISTANCE):.3f} F{FEED_RATE} "
                f"; sever the potentially still connected circle"
            )

            # go back from the severing cut
            lines.append(
                f"G1 X{ORIGIN_X + x_start:.3f} Y{(abs_y):.3f} F{TRAVEL_RATE} "
                f"; going back from the severing cut"
            )

    # Explicit normalize move back to the row's start (row should already be
    # here, but this keeps every row ending at a known, identical position -
    # also doubles as the "turn 90 degrees to the next row" transition move).
    lines.append(f"G1 X{row_start_x:.3f} Y{abs_y:.3f} F{TRAVEL_RATE} ; move to left edge before next row")


def compute_batch_extent(row_plan):
    """
    Given the fixed set of rows/segments that get cut every feed cycle,
    find the most-front and most-back machine Y coordinate the wire
    actually passes through while cutting.

    NOTE: a circle's G-code target Y is its centerline (abs_y) - the
    actual wire path bulges out to abs_y - r (lower sweep) and
    abs_y + r (upper sweep). So the true front/back extent of a cycle
    has to account for each row's largest circle radius, not just the
    row centerlines.
    """
    min_y = None
    max_y = None
    for actual_row_y, segments in row_plan:
        abs_y = ORIGIN_Y + actual_row_y
        radii = [seg[3] / 2.0 for seg in segments if seg[0] == "circle"]
        if not radii:
            continue
        r = max(radii)
        front = abs_y - r
        back = abs_y + r
        if min_y is None or front < min_y:
            min_y = front
        if max_y is None or back > max_y:
            max_y = back

    if min_y is None:
        raise ValueError(
            "No circles are cut in a single feed cycle - check CIRCLE_DIAMETER / "
            "PLANE_HEIGHT / PLANE_WIDTH / DIAMETER_OPTIONS."
        )
    return min_y, max_y


def generate_gcode():
    effective_width = PLANE_WIDTH - 2 * SQUISH_ROOM
    effective_height = PLANE_HEIGHT - 2 * SQUISH_ROOM

    if effective_width <= 0 or effective_height <= 0:
        raise ValueError(
            "SQUISH_ROOM is too large for the given PLANE_WIDTH/PLANE_HEIGHT "
            "- there's no usable area left to place circles in."
        )
    if ORIGIN_X < SQUISH_ROOM:
        print(
            f"WARNING: ORIGIN_X ({ORIGIN_X}) is less than SQUISH_ROOM "
            f"({SQUISH_ROOM}); left-edge overcut moves will go to a negative "
            f"machine X coordinate."
        )

    unusable_space = Z_Y_AXIS_OFFSET - Y_MAX
    if unusable_space < 0:
        raise ValueError(
            "Y_MAX is larger than Z_Y_AXIS_OFFSET - that would mean the Y axis "
            "could reach past the Z0 switch, which shouldn't be physically "
            "possible. Double check these two values."
        )
    if ORIGIN_Y + PLANE_HEIGHT > Y_MAX + 1e-9:
        print(
            f"WARNING: ORIGIN_Y + PLANE_HEIGHT ({ORIGIN_Y + PLANE_HEIGHT:g}) exceeds "
            f"Y_MAX ({Y_MAX:g}) - the cutting window reaches further than the "
            f"machine's absolute Y travel limit."
        )

    # The row/segment layout is IDENTICAL every feed cycle: the wire always
    # cuts in the same fixed machine-Y window near the front of the machine
    # (ORIGIN_Y .. ORIGIN_Y + PLANE_HEIGHT); it's the material underneath
    # that changes between cycles, courtesy of the Z-axis feeder. So it's
    # computed once and reused.
    rows = compute_rows(effective_height, CIRCLE_DIAMETER, PATTERN)

    row_plan = []
    for row_y, is_offset in rows:
        segments = compute_row_segments(effective_width, CIRCLE_DIAMETER, is_offset, DIAMETER_OPTIONS)
        segments = place_segments_in_plane(segments, PLANE_WIDTH, SQUISH_ROOM)
        actual_row_y = row_y + SQUISH_ROOM
        if row_has_circles(segments):
            row_plan.append((actual_row_y, segments))

    if not row_plan:
        raise ValueError(
            "No circles fit in a single feed cycle with the current "
            "PLANE_WIDTH / PLANE_HEIGHT / CIRCLE_DIAMETER / SQUISH_ROOM settings."
        )

    min_y, max_y = compute_batch_extent(row_plan)
    batch_span = max_y - min_y  # front-to-back reach of one cycle; also the push distance
    circles_per_cycle = sum(1 for _, segs in row_plan for s in segs if s[0] == "circle")

    lines = []
    lines.append("; Circle-packed cutting pattern with margin-filling and material feeding")
    lines.append(f"; 2D hot-wire cutting, X/Y only - {PATTERN} layout, row-sweep order")
    lines.append(f"; SQUISH_ROOM={SQUISH_ROOM}mm - circles inset from nominal edges, "
                  f"edge straight cuts overcut past nominal edges")
    lines.append(f"; Feeder (Z axis) shares its line of travel with the Y axis; the two "
                  f"homing switches are Z_Y_AXIS_OFFSET={Z_Y_AXIS_OFFSET:g}mm apart, Y+ "
                  f"and Z+ both pointing toward the middle. Y_MAX={Y_MAX:g}mm is the hard "
                  f"Y travel limit, leaving UNUSABLE_SPACE={unusable_space:.3f}mm of feed "
                  f"travel before the wire can reach the material at all.")
    lines.append(f"; Every feed cycle cuts the same {len(row_plan)}-row layout "
                  f"({circles_per_cycle} circles) in the fixed window "
                  f"Y{ORIGIN_Y:.3f}-Y{(ORIGIN_Y + PLANE_HEIGHT):.3f}; the wire's actual "
                  f"front/back reach is Y{min_y:.3f}-Y{max_y:.3f} "
                  f"(span {batch_span:.3f}mm), which is how far the feeder pushes fresh "
                  f"material in after each cycle.")
    lines.append("G21 ; units = mm")
    lines.append("G90 ; absolute positioning")
    lines.append("G17 ; XY plane for arcs")
    lines.append(f"G1 X0.000 Y0.000 F{TRAVEL_RATE} ; wire clear at home before the first feed")

    z_position = 0.0
    material_fed = 0.0
    total_circles = 0
    cycle = 0

    while True:
        if cycle == 0:
            feed_amount = unusable_space + ORIGIN_Y
            feed_note = "clear unusable space, then reach safety distance"
        else:
            feed_amount = batch_span
            feed_note = "push cut material clear, bring in fresh material"

        # Stop if there isn't enough material left in the sheet for another
        # full, safe cutting cycle - reserving SQUISH_ROOM at the very tail
        # end of the sheet, mirroring the inset used at its leading edge.
        if material_fed + feed_amount + SQUISH_ROOM > MATERIAL_LENGTH + 1e-6:
            lines.append(
                f"; {MATERIAL_LENGTH - material_fed:.3f}mm of material left - not enough "
                f"for another full feed cycle, stopping"
            )
            break

        z_position += feed_amount
        material_fed += feed_amount
        lines.append(
            f"G1 Z{z_position:.3f} F{PUSH_RATE} ; cycle {cycle + 1}: {feed_note} "
            f"({feed_amount:.3f}mm)"
        )

        for actual_row_y, segments in row_plan:
            emit_row(lines, actual_row_y, segments)
        total_circles += circles_per_cycle

        lines.append(
            f"G1 X0.000 Y0.000 F{TRAVEL_RATE} ; cycle {cycle + 1} done - return to home, "
            f"clear the area for the next feed"
        )

        cycle += 1

    if cycle == 0:
        raise ValueError(
            "MATERIAL_LENGTH is too short to even complete a single feed cycle "
            "given UNUSABLE_SPACE, ORIGIN_Y and the cutting window - nothing was cut."
        )

    lines.append("M2 ; end of program")

    return "\n".join(lines), total_circles, cycle, batch_span, unusable_space


if __name__ == "__main__":
    gcode, total_circles, cycles, batch_span, unusable_space = generate_gcode()
    with open(OUTPUT_FILE, "w") as f:
        f.write(gcode)
    print(f"G-code written to {OUTPUT_FILE}")
    print(f"{PATTERN} layout: {total_circles} circles across {cycles} feed cycles, "
          f"main diameter {CIRCLE_DIAMETER}mm, sheet {PLANE_WIDTH}x{MATERIAL_LENGTH}mm")
    print(f"Diameter options checked for margins: {DIAMETER_OPTIONS}")
    print(f"Unusable feed space before the wire can reach material: {unusable_space:.3f}mm")
    print(f"Feed distance per cycle after the first: {batch_span:.3f}mm")